{
  "id": "c12",
  "title": "Tables and Sorting",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c12-l1",
      "title": "Two-Dimensional Arrays",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array of arrays is a table: int grid[3][4]; has 3 rows with 4 columns each. grid[1][2] is row 1, column 2, counting from 0 as always.",
        "Two nested loops walk through a table: the outer one over the rows, the inner one over the columns.",
        "Unlike FORTRAN, C stores tables row by row: grid[0][0], grid[0][1], grid[0][2] … then the next row."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int t[3][4];",
          "    int r, c;",
          "    for (r = 0; r < 3; r++) {",
          "        for (c = 0; c < 4; c++) {",
          "            t[r][c] = (r + 1) * (c + 1);",
          "            printf(\"%4d\", t[r][c]);",
          "        }",
          "        printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    }",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "   1   2   3   4",
          "   2   4   6   8",
          "   3   6   9  12"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
