    {
      "id": "c12-l2",
      "title": "Sorting",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Sorting was one of the most common jobs for early computers. The simplest way is bubble sort: walk through the array, and whenever two neighbors are in the wrong order, swap them. Repeat until nothing moves.",
        "A swap needs a spare variable: t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;",
        "Bubble sort is slow for big arrays. Unix's qsort used a much faster method, quicksort, invented by Tony Hoare in 1959."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int a[6] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 7, 3};",
          "    int i, j, t;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)",
          "        for (j = 0; j < 5 - i; j++)",
          "            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {",
          "                t = a[j];",
          "                a[j] = a[j + 1];",
          "                a[j + 1] = t;",
          "            }",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 6; i++)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "1 2 3 5 7 9 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does bubble sort compare?",
          "options": [
            "Two neighbors at a time",
            "The first and the last element",
            "Every element with 0",
            "Random pairs"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Big values bubble towards the end, one swap at a time."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does a swap need t?",
          "options": [
            "To keep one value while the other is copied over it",
            "C requires three variables",
            "To count the swaps",
            "It doesn't"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Without t, a[j] = a[j + 1] would lose the old a[j]."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Sort from biggest to smallest instead.",
          "code": [
            "        if (a[j] ___ a[j + 1]) {"
          ],
          "options": [
            "<",
            ">",
            "==",
            ">="
          ],
          "answer": "<",
          "explanation": "Swap when the left one is smaller."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "After one full pass of bubble sort, which value is surely in its place?",
          "options": [
            "The biggest, at the end",
            "The smallest, at the start",
            "The middle one",
            "None"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The biggest value bubbles all the way to the end."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This sort crashes. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int a[4] = {3, 1, 4, 2};",
            "    int j, t;",
            "    for (j = 0; j < 4; j++)",
            "        if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {",
            "            t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;",
            "        }",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 5,
          "explanation": "With j up to 3, a[j + 1] is a[4], outside the array. The loop must stop at j < 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Who invented quicksort?",
          "options": [
            "Tony Hoare",
            "Dennis Ritchie",
            "Grace Hopper",
            "Ken Thompson"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Hoare came up with it in 1959. It's still one of the fastest ways to sort."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c12-l3",
      "title": "Searching",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To find a value, check the elements one by one: linear search. Stop as soon as you find it, and remember where.",
        "If nothing matches, the search needs an answer for \"not found\". -1 is the usual choice, because no real index is negative.",
        "In a sorted array, there's a much faster way: binary search. Look in the middle, then throw away the half that can't contain the value. 1000 elements need only 10 looks."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int a[7] = {3, 8, 15, 23, 42, 57, 91};",
          "    int want = 42, i, pos = -1;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 7; i++)",
          "        if (a[i] == want) {",
          "            pos = i;",
          "            break;",
          "        }",
          "    printf(\"%d is at %d\\n\", want, pos);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "42 is at 4"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c12-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does linear search do?",
          "options": [
            "Checks the elements one after the other",
            "Looks only in the middle",
            "Sorts first",
            "Guesses"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Simple, and works on unsorted arrays."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why is -1 used for \"not found\"?",
          "options": [
            "No real index is negative",
            "-1 means error in printf",
            "Arrays end at -1",
            "It's the last element"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The caller can check if (pos == -1)."
        },
        {
          "id": "c12-l3-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many looks does binary search need for 1000 sorted elements, at most?",
